#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192300 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1fdf4f96-f679-57b8-96aa-7e9c34b986b8" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability Over an Unbounded Interval"}] :question/id #uuid "3f141210-8194-5346-9e2a-c47f98bb3443" :question/math-academy-id "e-10840" :question/problem "Compute $P\\left( X > 8 \\right)$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062347/assets/cc8c2867f9ef99a8c476feeb319e03a3a479dd23f04e546bf1e5240daa4ea0ac.png)" :question/worked-solution "If a continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x),$ then \n\n$$\nP(a <    X <    b) = \\int_a^b f(x) \\, \\textrm dx.\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>8) & =P(8<X<\\infty ) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{8}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{8}^{\\infty }5{e}^{-5x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-{e}^{-5x}]}_{8}^{\\infty } \\\\  & ={e}^{-40}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192301 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd8455e1-46f3-5956-80ec-e6753cc005a7" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for a Constant Given a PDF"}] :question/id #uuid "d40a67bd-7d98-58fb-b4a1-7a556e91556a" :question/math-academy-id "e-10851" :question/problem "Solve for $k$ given that the following function is a probability density function:\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062347/assets/0ba485cc9b536bd1a7cd18f37c61d269515b2bcb621cabfa55d6a567d53144c6.png)" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ defined on a set $S$ to be a valid probability density function, it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x) \\geq 0$ for all $x \\in S$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{S} f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 1$\n\nIn our case, for the function $f(x) = \\dfrac{k}{(4x+1)^3}$ on the interval $x \\geq 0,$ the second condition states that\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{\\infty} \\dfrac{k}{(4x+1)^3} \\, \\textrm dx = 1.\n$$\n\nComputing the integral on the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{k}{(4x+1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-{[\\frac{k}{8(4x+1)^{2}}]}_{0}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =\\frac{k}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this result back into the first equation and solving for $k,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{k}{8} = 1 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad k = 8.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192355 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "441a1de0-dc64-59fe-99a8-0ab502841425" :knowledge-point/title "Checking Whether a Given Function Is a Valid PDF"}] :question/id #uuid "5f988175-cdd9-5ddf-94b5-3b995dc75fce" :question/math-academy-id "e-12329" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062347/assets/d6ad9cbcf369609541984bb88a414653161a9ed723ec2637d6e73cc81b62a238.png)\n\nGiven the function $f(x)$ above, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x) \\geq 0$ for all $x \\geq 3$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{3}^{\\infty} f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x = 1$\n\n- $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x) \\geq 0$ for all $x \\in S$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{S} f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for $x \\geq 3,$ we have $f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{(x-2)^2} \\geq 0.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(x-2)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }(x-2)^{-2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{x-2}\\,{|}_{3}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =-(0-1) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statements I and II are true, the first and second conditions are satisfied. So, $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343630 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1fdf4f96-f679-57b8-96aa-7e9c34b986b8" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability Over an Unbounded Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1487d4df-e3bd-5585-b21c-dfe4c3398ed5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343632} {:answer/id #uuid "e896bd42-ce89-5980-abed-680138a7c92f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{e}^{-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343633} {:answer/id #uuid "de704ba8-b32b-5116-8c83-4ff57be8cb22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343634} {:answer/id #uuid "4f6d9e2f-a6ca-56f8-b684-0969fd6e8bd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343635} {:answer/id #uuid "6ff5d366-f3f0-58f2-ab45-399c4d3a5a82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-\\frac{1}{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343636}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4f6d9e2f-a6ca-56f8-b684-0969fd6e8bd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343635} :answer-field/id #uuid "c802a245-15a1-5149-9a06-be5c2b0a3b42" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343631}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "87ab5e1a-40f2-585e-8f36-c410606655e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-126016" :question/problem "Compute $P(X\\ge 3)$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-\\frac{1}{3}x},\\, & x\\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\, & x<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "If a continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x),$ then \n\n$$\nP(a<X<b)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 3) & =P(3\\le X<\\infty ) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-\\frac{1}{3}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-{e}^{-\\frac{1}{3}x}]}_{3}^{\\infty } \\\\  & ={e}^{-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343637 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1fdf4f96-f679-57b8-96aa-7e9c34b986b8" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability Over an Unbounded Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e039e7a-2583-5028-8302-700c2b53fa1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-0.1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343639} {:answer/id #uuid "d1282e1c-7dea-5f41-9355-19945b7c2a87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-0.1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343640} {:answer/id #uuid "0c590bfc-8376-5255-a29e-4f09fe6485dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343641} {:answer/id #uuid "04518b46-838e-5873-bb77-7410af4ef989" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-0.3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343642} {:answer/id #uuid "747860c1-d1fe-50f4-a29e-7839415e08a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{0.1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343643}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e039e7a-2583-5028-8302-700c2b53fa1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-0.1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343639} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec131ce1-6f7a-5d0d-bdef-856d2c27772b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343638}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fb744e8a-ff2d-5960-83aa-70c9996a145a" :question/math-academy-id "q-126018" :question/problem "Compute $P(X>1)$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}0.1{e}^{-0.1x},\\, & x\\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\, & x<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "If a continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x),$ then \n\n$$\nP(a<X<b)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>1) & =P(1<X<\\infty ) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }0.1{e}^{-0.1x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-{e}^{-0.1x}]}_{1}^{\\infty } \\\\  & ={e}^{-0.1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343644 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1fdf4f96-f679-57b8-96aa-7e9c34b986b8" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability Over an Unbounded Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d6061aec-ac09-5016-a963-b7282c213250" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343646} {:answer/id #uuid "791c624b-10bd-5dbd-bc9e-42b30f2fff9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343647} {:answer/id #uuid "bbf31511-480a-57f7-9483-3c605b3c70ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343648} {:answer/id #uuid "51a7f16e-7442-5559-8f3f-524f3bd117ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343649} {:answer/id #uuid "ff65de56-572e-5a0d-9118-63fe1e6e6cbe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{-4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343650}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d6061aec-ac09-5016-a963-b7282c213250" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343646} :answer-field/id #uuid "23211f8b-aa86-54be-8e78-65e6a541a77c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343645}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2191f3b5-f79c-5fb9-91eb-b95fdad50ac6" :question/math-academy-id "q-126032" :question/problem "Compute $P(X\\ge 2)$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{2-2x},\\, & x\\ge 1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "If a continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x),$ then \n\n$$\nP(a<X<b)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 2) & =P(2\\le X<\\infty ) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }2{e}^{2-2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-{e}^{2-2x}]}_{2}^{\\infty } \\\\  & ={e}^{-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192966 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "441a1de0-dc64-59fe-99a8-0ab502841425" :knowledge-point/title "Checking Whether a Given Function Is a Valid PDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d852c985-cbe1-5f90-8568-29934c457ee7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186190646} {:answer/id #uuid "06935761-edc1-5300-a537-ad9758b0de55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186190647} {:answer/id #uuid "4feb4af0-61e9-5d19-b099-eccf50a1b899" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186190648} {:answer/id #uuid "50c8f9ce-a318-54ed-a572-a51798cbc440" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186190649} {:answer/id #uuid "67b2e705-fb9c-541b-8f07-7fb4f822d8e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186190650}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "67b2e705-fb9c-541b-8f07-7fb4f822d8e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186190650} :answer-field/id #uuid "03002436-b7cf-5314-9723-b24d1886cdd1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186190645}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e8f7202f-949a-5544-b1eb-75cc6a08bdaf" :question/math-academy-id "q-154696" :question/problem "$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{e}^{1-x},\\, & x\\ge 1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the function $f(x)$ above, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\ge 1$\n- ${\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n- $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for $x\\ge 1$, we have $f(x)={e}^{1-x}\\ge 0.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{e}^{1-x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-{e}^{1-x}]}_{1}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =0-(-{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statements I and II are true, the first and second conditions are satisfied. So, $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343651 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "441a1de0-dc64-59fe-99a8-0ab502841425" :knowledge-point/title "Checking Whether a Given Function Is a Valid PDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11c2f7a8-e0f6-54e9-a62c-788e77ce82dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343653} {:answer/id #uuid "d2200014-ef31-5fc1-8821-00e2cd597fd0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343654} {:answer/id #uuid "f5beb9b2-e403-541c-94a4-2b15238b4fdd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343655} {:answer/id #uuid "0e333a2e-4569-5f23-b8d5-8f3c8ed28a7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343656} {:answer/id #uuid "f5bfded8-137f-569d-949e-481549c44b65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343657}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f5beb9b2-e403-541c-94a4-2b15238b4fdd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343655} :answer-field/id #uuid "50c0df9a-5f39-59c9-9049-3b24e784608c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343652}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "576ea109-3051-59ce-a7ba-67047d37a95a" :question/math-academy-id "q-160447" :question/problem "$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x/2},\\, & x\\le 0, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the function $f(x)$ above, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\le 0$\n- ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n- $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for $x\\le 0,$ we have $f(x)={e}^{x/2}\\ge 0.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{e}^{x/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{e}^{x/2}\\,{|}_{-\\infty }^{0} \\\\  & =2({e}^{0}-0) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, $f(x)$ is not a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343658 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "441a1de0-dc64-59fe-99a8-0ab502841425" :knowledge-point/title "Checking Whether a Given Function Is a Valid PDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "de602220-a4c2-5ee6-8a1c-9b1364471cba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343660} {:answer/id #uuid "8c36ecf4-ce53-5f85-858d-d4752fc8162b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343661} {:answer/id #uuid "550a56cd-0e38-5a5f-9c96-a336a635b67e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343662} {:answer/id #uuid "289fd94c-d897-5b3c-9f9e-d9dc40c69681" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343663} {:answer/id #uuid "acf8bbfb-13a0-5fb4-8d9e-add9d122cf04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343664}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "550a56cd-0e38-5a5f-9c96-a336a635b67e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343662} :answer-field/id #uuid "57833a01-d1ff-54d5-aa39-1e06e0d775aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343659}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0680bfff-51f7-58c3-9dbe-9713d602a7d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-160450" :question/problem "$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8}{{x}^{5}},\\, & x\\ge 1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the function $f(x)$ above, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\ge 1$\n- ${\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n- $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for $x\\ge 1,$ we have $f(x)=\\frac{8}{{x}^{5}}\\ge 0.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{8}{{x}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{{x}^{4}}\\,{|}_{1}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =-(0-2) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, $f(x)$ is not a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343665 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "441a1de0-dc64-59fe-99a8-0ab502841425" :knowledge-point/title "Checking Whether a Given Function Is a Valid PDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "af0c8623-4908-516b-9e9d-dfe7b43591b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343667} {:answer/id #uuid "b98f0199-cd39-5f46-9032-34fd4c4c28a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343668} {:answer/id #uuid "c762d5ab-1a2e-5a0b-a653-e611c868b48f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343669} {:answer/id #uuid "0727cff0-8402-5226-9c30-edd3d293ff89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343670} {:answer/id #uuid "54cd70d8-b608-59d0-ad85-3beaf2aa4823" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343671}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c762d5ab-1a2e-5a0b-a653-e611c868b48f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343669} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b6210cb-456a-574b-9d3f-31582abbcc58" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343666}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9d8aae59-53b9-5f80-8db0-b8e59b98bd0a" :question/math-academy-id "q-160455" :question/problem "$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{1-2x},\\, & x\\ge 1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the function $f(x)$ above, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\ge 1$\n- ${\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n- $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for $x\\ge 1,$ we have $f(x)=2{e}^{1-2x}\\ge 0.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }2{e}^{1-2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{1-2x}\\,{|}_{1}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =-(0-{e}^{-1}) \\\\  & ={e}^{-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, $f(x)$ is not a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343672 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "441a1de0-dc64-59fe-99a8-0ab502841425" :knowledge-point/title "Checking Whether a Given Function Is a Valid PDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f7c7d997-9138-54e9-8a49-acaae41087cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343674} {:answer/id #uuid "ce3b999f-1664-5ae5-b147-57b350c7085f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343675} {:answer/id #uuid "d1b4a1c4-1508-597e-84bd-328f32a39659" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343676} {:answer/id #uuid "e8b0162d-eb99-59e5-900f-fbc27d53cc2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343677} {:answer/id #uuid "3c778c8f-f3bb-5223-9289-d976e7a3b56b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343678}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f7c7d997-9138-54e9-8a49-acaae41087cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343674} :answer-field/id #uuid "93534edc-2253-5472-b70f-366595869299" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343673}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f8f4a5ce-40a5-520d-b284-4547a3fc3317" :question/math-academy-id "q-160459" :question/problem "$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{(3x+1)^{4}},\\, & x\\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the function $f(x)$ above, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\ge 0$\n- ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n- $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for $x\\ge 0,$ we have $f(x)=\\frac{9}{(3x+1)^{4}}\\ge 0.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{9}{(3x+1)^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{(3x+1)^{3}}\\,{|}_{0}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =-(0-1) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statements I and II are true, the first and second conditions are satisfied. So, $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343679 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd8455e1-46f3-5956-80ec-e6753cc005a7" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for a Constant Given a PDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b3bc02e-7ff1-55aa-8c09-1ca2afa1ed0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343681} {:answer/id #uuid "3aa97bad-0541-5aea-9ef2-a1670572c669" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343682} {:answer/id #uuid "fd2c70ef-e6d4-56c4-b25c-c98358523fa8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343683} {:answer/id #uuid "d9d829d8-74d2-506e-b13e-e526b9f40473" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343684} {:answer/id #uuid "6c39349d-4f94-5f50-9e08-5a9124bb68a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343685}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d9d829d8-74d2-506e-b13e-e526b9f40473" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343684} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c3a0a15-9719-51ca-8483-7f960c2b8bc6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343680}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2223100b-9916-59a8-976f-154591c025fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-160463" :question/problem "Solve for $k$ given that the following function is a probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{k}{(x+3)^{7}},\\, & x\\ge -2, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ defined on a set $S$ to be a valid probability density function, it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nIn our case, for the function $f(x)=\\frac{k}{(x+3)^{7}}$ on the interval $x\\ge -2,$ the second condition states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{\\infty }\\frac{k}{(x+3)^{7}}\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nComputing the integral on the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{\\infty }\\frac{k}{(x+3)^{7}}\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{k}{6(x+3)^{6}}\\,{|}_{-2}^{\\infty }=\\frac{k}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this result back into the first equation and solving for $k,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{k}{6}=1\\,⟹\\,k=6.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343686 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd8455e1-46f3-5956-80ec-e6753cc005a7" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for a Constant Given a PDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "82ac609f-8257-58f7-bc67-5c58f00cc248" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343688} {:answer/id #uuid "b71fb08c-533a-547e-b655-0e4cf8f01589" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343689} {:answer/id #uuid "44ff6b02-fb46-515c-a5e6-6c599deb4647" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343690} {:answer/id #uuid "5509e390-3a9d-5bf2-a2d1-38c3bc97475f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343691} {:answer/id #uuid "424e2858-e8ab-5e9e-accc-55d9dea2f0ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343692}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b71fb08c-533a-547e-b655-0e4cf8f01589" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343689} :answer-field/id #uuid "5dadf009-419d-5129-8b4b-9ebb196f1b44" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343687}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cf3e1e26-918b-5252-8cf9-24dbf74b090c" :question/math-academy-id "q-160466" :question/problem "Solve for $k$ given that the following function is a probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}k{e}^{1-2x},\\, & x\\ge 1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ defined on a set $S$ to be a valid probability density function, it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nIn our case, for the function $f(x)=k{e}^{1-2x}$ on the interval $x\\ge 1,$ the second condition states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }k{e}^{1-2x}\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nComputing the integral on the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }k{e}^{1-2x}\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{k}{2}{e}^{1-2x}\\,{|}_{1}^{\\infty }=\\frac{k}{2e}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this result back into the first equation and solving for $k,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{k}{2e}=1\\,⟹\\,k=2e.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343693 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd8455e1-46f3-5956-80ec-e6753cc005a7" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for a Constant Given a PDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fe6d88e2-d1d8-553c-8342-0a80ac1615cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{e}^{-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343695} {:answer/id #uuid "6cd70fc6-5d73-58a7-bf28-b67e581cdd32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343696} {:answer/id #uuid "dc42ed7f-922f-5b9f-80a6-14a060310d87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343697} {:answer/id #uuid "810e6bf6-08cb-5b29-8a13-098f135356b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343698} {:answer/id #uuid "1e5f1376-6a1e-5351-beea-d0f711e606a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343699}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1e5f1376-6a1e-5351-beea-d0f711e606a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343699} :answer-field/id #uuid "39041751-656e-51f4-9429-ba9805f5e43e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343694}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "72cfc3d8-5a3c-5ad7-8fd8-90e82328cc82" :question/math-academy-id "q-160469" :question/problem "Solve for $k$ given that the following function is a probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}k{e}^{2-x},\\, & x\\ge -1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ defined on a set $S$ to be a valid probability density function, it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nIn our case, for the function $f(x)=k{e}^{2-x}$ on the interval $x\\ge -1,$ the second condition states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{\\infty }k{e}^{2-x}\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nComputing the integral on the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{\\infty }k{e}^{2-x}\\,\\text{d}x=-k{e}^{2-x}\\,{|}_{-1}^{\\infty }=k{e}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this result back into the first equation and solving for $k,$ we get\n\n$$\nk{e}^{3}=1\\,⟹\\,k={e}^{-3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193217 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1fdf4f96-f679-57b8-96aa-7e9c34b986b8" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability Over an Unbounded Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "93ca28c7-c073-5b0b-8f87-21a719911816" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{-8/5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186192091} {:answer/id #uuid "ae3dca22-bcb2-5164-8131-6b67a668b9c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{-16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186192092} {:answer/id #uuid "c5e31b99-85f0-5712-ab3e-143e3dc8d053" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{-8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186192093} {:answer/id #uuid "d078ccfa-2ddd-56b1-8a92-395358194ed2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{-5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186192094} {:answer/id #uuid "327bf6af-ba56-5290-a6bc-2ea947c7f655" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{-40}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186192095}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "327bf6af-ba56-5290-a6bc-2ea947c7f655" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{-40}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186192095} :answer-field/id #uuid "08b6c946-9843-582c-8c4a-79d6b05fb010" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186192090}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fb604529-990b-53e6-81c3-68ba39e9f14b" :question/math-academy-id "q-86914" :question/problem "Compute $P(X\\ge 5)$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}8{e}^{-8x},\\, & x\\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\, & x<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "If a continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x),$ then \n\n$$\nP(a<X<b)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 5) & =P(5\\le X<\\infty ) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{5}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{5}^{\\infty }8{e}^{-8x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-{e}^{-8x}]}_{5}^{\\infty } \\\\  & ={e}^{-40}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193218 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd8455e1-46f3-5956-80ec-e6753cc005a7" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for a Constant Given a PDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a8b74669-c7e2-5366-a875-96f6cef35eec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186192097} {:answer/id #uuid "ad423402-2b12-500d-a4ca-8f17ea92e55c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186192098} {:answer/id #uuid "ddad6f9e-f0f8-588c-b743-8bc6d51253ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186192099} {:answer/id #uuid "e79fd51d-60b7-54f7-b520-8426846cd763" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186192100} {:answer/id #uuid "e9d23dda-711f-5b4e-9467-2a9ebc0235dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186192101}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e9d23dda-711f-5b4e-9467-2a9ebc0235dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186192101} :answer-field/id #uuid "c6945ac0-3988-547a-aac6-46452ad607de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186192096}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b4face19-aab4-54c4-8e7d-e848c1b716b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-86917" :question/problem "Solve for $k$ given that the following function is a probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}k{e}^{-3x},\\, & x\\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ defined on a set $S$ to be a valid probability density function, it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nIn our case, for the function $f(x)=k{e}^{-3x}$ on the interval $0\\le x<\\infty ,$ the second condition states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }k{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nComputing the integral on the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }k{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x={[-\\frac{k}{3}{e}^{-3x}]}_{0}^{\\infty }=\\frac{k}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this result back into the first equation and solving for $k,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{k}{3}=1\\,⟹\\,k=3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343700 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1fdf4f96-f679-57b8-96aa-7e9c34b986b8" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability Over an Unbounded Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9736207c-e353-5335-9326-acdc04967c29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343702} {:answer/id #uuid "3f5b8585-faf2-5a24-bf82-bfa372632f6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343703} {:answer/id #uuid "6d3f9c4b-863b-544a-9960-4043155fc2ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343704} {:answer/id #uuid "9d594cc2-82ab-5d72-aba5-2c2f27bdbf9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-1/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343705} {:answer/id #uuid "e7750519-78a4-59eb-8a98-4e5189db8550" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343706}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6d3f9c4b-863b-544a-9960-4043155fc2ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343704} :answer-field/id #uuid "65b0293b-32c4-5c89-8f3c-e0b208f894f3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343701}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c18e7418-d781-59f4-9d8f-d01f265d2cb9" :question/math-academy-id "q-87219" :question/problem "Compute $P(X>6)$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{-3x},\\, & x\\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\, & x<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "If a continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x),$ then \n\n$$\nP(a<X<b)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>6) & =P(6<X<\\infty ) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{6}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{6}^{\\infty }3{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-{e}^{-3x}]}_{6}^{\\infty } \\\\  & ={e}^{-18}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343707 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd8455e1-46f3-5956-80ec-e6753cc005a7" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for a Constant Given a PDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f83266b9-42b8-5611-baaf-8ffcea03af4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343709} {:answer/id #uuid "2fcf39c4-bdd6-5dcf-8b28-98ea770b549e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343710} {:answer/id #uuid "76b3663b-d6ce-585f-beca-518b4811543b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343711} {:answer/id #uuid "2ab882f7-2e87-5a95-96b1-f58ccd3d5fc6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343712} {:answer/id #uuid "21823a6b-cad3-5408-9674-9c38d424e295" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343713}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "21823a6b-cad3-5408-9674-9c38d424e295" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343713} :answer-field/id #uuid "60303679-9e1b-53e2-9f58-a6090507b968" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343708}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "23fa3ed5-67fd-5567-92e1-2865c6e59cbf" :question/math-academy-id "q-87238" :question/problem "Solve for $k$ given that the following function is a probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}k{e}^{-5x},\\, & x\\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ defined on a set $S$ to be a valid probability density function, it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nIn our case, for the function $f(x)=k{e}^{-5x}$ on the interval $0\\le x<\\infty ,$ the second condition states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }k{e}^{-5x}\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nComputing the integral on the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }k{e}^{-5x}\\,\\text{d}x={[-\\frac{k}{5}{e}^{-5x}]}_{0}^{\\infty }=\\frac{k}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this result back into the first equation and solving for $k,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{k}{5}=1\\,⟹\\,k=5.\n$$"}]}
